WEBVTT

00:00:01.50 --> 00:00:06.14
This is journey thirty four of Hewn, the software factory: use keyboard and

00:00:06.14 --> 00:00:07.44
assistive technology.

00:00:07.44 --> 00:00:11.46
Someone who works with a keyboard alone, or with a screen reader,

00:00:11.46 --> 00:00:15.11
has to be able to do everything an Owner does with a mouse.

00:00:15.11 --> 00:00:20.06
This video shows a direction submitted and cancelled without touching the mouse,

00:00:20.06 --> 00:00:23.16
what the screen reader hears when a stage changes,

00:00:23.16 --> 00:00:25.69
and the tests that hold every page to it.

00:00:26.84 --> 00:00:28.90
The requirement has four parts.

00:00:28.90 --> 00:00:34.28
Every page, in every state, passes axe core with no serious or critical problems.

00:00:34.28 --> 00:00:37.80
Everything can be reached and used from the keyboard,

00:00:37.80 --> 00:00:40.72
with a visible focus indicator and no traps.

00:00:40.72 --> 00:00:44.50
Stage changes are announced through a polite live region,

00:00:44.50 --> 00:00:47.76
at most once every five seconds for each project.

00:00:47.76 --> 00:00:51.67
And when the user prefers reduced motion, nothing animates.

00:00:52.82 --> 00:00:55.73
Here is the whole journey on the keyboard.

00:00:55.73 --> 00:01:00.24
The Owner tabs to New direction in the sidebar and presses Enter.

00:01:00.24 --> 00:01:03.15
They tab into Name and type it, then Idea,

00:01:03.15 --> 00:01:06.20
then tab to Start direction and press Enter.

00:01:06.20 --> 00:01:08.76
Every stop shows the blue focus ring.

00:01:09.11 --> 00:01:13.70
On the project page, they tab to the Idea stage and press Enter,

00:01:13.70 --> 00:01:17.57
then move along the detail's tabs with the arrow keys.

00:01:17.57 --> 00:01:20.08
They tab to Cancel and press Enter.

00:01:20.08 --> 00:01:21.73
The dialog takes focus.

00:01:21.73 --> 00:01:25.17
Escape closes it, and focus goes back to Cancel.

00:01:25.17 --> 00:01:30.19
Enter opens it again, Tab reaches Cancel direction, and Enter cancels.

00:01:31.34 --> 00:01:35.20
When a stage changes, the shell's live region says so, politely,

00:01:35.20 --> 00:01:38.93
so it waits for the screen reader to finish what it's reading.

00:01:38.93 --> 00:01:42.73
On the project page it says the stage, its name and its status.

00:01:42.73 --> 00:01:45.51
On Projects it starts with the project's name.

00:01:45.51 --> 00:01:49.06
A project gets at most one announcement every five seconds,

00:01:49.06 --> 00:01:53.29
and when several changes arrive in that time, only the latest is said.

00:01:53.29 --> 00:01:58.35
The clip shows each announcement on screen as the live region gives it to the screen

00:01:58.35 --> 00:01:58.77
reader.

00:01:59.92 --> 00:02:05.58
Axe core runs on every page in every state the app has: loaded, loading, failed,

00:02:05.58 --> 00:02:09.82
empty, for an Owner and a Viewer, and with its dialogs open.

00:02:09.82 --> 00:02:15.76
The new sweep holds each page on its loading request, then fails it, and scans both.

00:02:15.76 --> 00:02:20.79
Across those scans there are no violations at all, not even minor ones.

00:02:21.94 --> 00:02:27.37
With reduced motion on, the spinners stand still, the dialog appears without fading,

00:02:27.37 --> 00:02:29.38
and the drawer is simply there.

00:02:29.38 --> 00:02:34.75
The motion tokens drop to zero, and a test checks that nothing on the page runs for

00:02:34.75 --> 00:02:37.40
longer than a hundredth of a millisecond.

00:02:38.55 --> 00:02:42.70
The project page used to keep its own live region, and Projects,

00:02:42.70 --> 00:02:45.49
which also changes rows live, said nothing.

00:02:45.49 --> 00:02:50.68
Now one service, the status announcer, sits behind a single region in the shell.

00:02:50.68 --> 00:02:53.40
A project's first change goes out at once.

00:02:53.40 --> 00:02:58.07
Later changes wait for that project's five seconds, and the latest wins.

00:02:58.07 --> 00:03:03.06
Each project has its own clock, so one busy project never holds back another.

00:03:03.41 --> 00:03:06.44
The project page feeds it each stage change.

00:03:06.44 --> 00:03:10.22
Projects feeds it when a row's stage or status changes.

00:03:10.57 --> 00:03:14.30
The class diagram shows the shell rendering the message,

00:03:14.30 --> 00:03:16.83
with both pages feeding the announcer.

00:03:16.83 --> 00:03:22.30
The sequence shows three quick changes for one project becoming two announcements,

00:03:22.30 --> 00:03:23.56
five seconds apart.

00:03:24.71 --> 00:03:28.91
The announcer's unit tests use a fake clock: first change at once,

00:03:28.91 --> 00:03:32.16
the next held for five seconds, the latest winning,

00:03:32.16 --> 00:03:34.33
and each project on its own clock.

00:03:34.33 --> 00:03:37.51
They failed first, because there was no announcer.

00:03:37.86 --> 00:03:43.18
The pipeline test runs two projects with workers and records the live region on

00:03:43.18 --> 00:03:43.78
Projects.

00:03:43.78 --> 00:03:47.89
Before the change it failed, because the region didn't exist.

00:03:47.89 --> 00:03:52.26
Now each project's announcements are at least five seconds apart.

00:03:52.61 --> 00:03:57.71
The keyboard sweep tabs through thirteen pages, about three hundred stops,

00:03:57.71 --> 00:04:02.60
checking every stop for the focus ring and that focus comes back round.

00:04:02.60 --> 00:04:06.25
With the focus styles removed, it reports every stop.

00:04:06.25 --> 00:04:07.35
With them, none.

00:04:07.70 --> 00:04:12.75
The loading and failure scans found one gap: Settings had no loading state.

00:04:12.75 --> 00:04:17.72
It showed the configured defaults, editable, until the saved ones arrived.

00:04:17.72 --> 00:04:22.70
Now it shows the skeletons from its mock, and a visual test compares them.

00:04:23.85 --> 00:04:26.02
Journey thirty four is complete.

00:04:26.02 --> 00:04:31.50
Every page works from the keyboard, with a focus ring at every stop and no traps.

00:04:31.50 --> 00:04:34.96
Stage changes are announced from one polite region,

00:04:34.96 --> 00:04:40.24
once every five seconds for each project, on the project page and on Projects.

00:04:40.24 --> 00:04:45.11
Axe core finds nothing in any state, and reduced motion means no motion.

00:04:45.11 --> 00:04:47.21
Next comes journey thirty five.
